the phrase that i use is a metaphor it's  a figure of speech in fact  john mcdonnell used the same phrase to  gordon brown in 2007  and i think as you know some of your  former colleagues on the sun i think you  worked on the sun with kevin schofield  have pointed out  that this is a figure of speech and it's  this has been used to deflect from the  real issue  and the real issue here is that the  extraordinary  has become the new normal in the labour  party and it's not normal  it is not normal what we are seeing  where you have a labour mp  well respected on both sides of the  house like frankfield for example  resigning the whip because of the  culture in the party and because of  anti-semitism it's not normal  to have two jewish labour mps give  impassioned  moving speeches in the house of commons  outlining in detail  the racism to which they have been  subject including by supporters and  members  of the labour party it is not normal for  iranian state tv to live stream and  broadcast a labour party meeting at  which an  excellent local mp like joan ryan has a  motion passed against her  and let's not forget all of the members  of parliament that i'm talking about  were democratically elected by their  constituents just a year ago  and it would appear now that people are  seeking to help them from office  now my my biggest gripe about all of  this in many respects is  distracting from the big issue for me  which is brexit we're facing the biggest  issue  since the second world war in this  country which is going to do  disproportionate damage and already is  doing disproportionate damage to lower  middle income families in this country  who are represented by the labor party  all of this  distracts from us being able to properly  do our job  on brexit we'll talk about brexit later  in the interview but i'm keen just to  focus for a little bit  on the labour party in issues within the  labour party you were talking there  about  jewish mps do you think that the labour  party  is effectively institutionally racist  yes i do if you look at the  definition of institutional racism as  outlined by sir william mcpherson in the  mcpherson report  and the mcpherson inquiry produced the  institutional racism definition  the labour party is beyond doubt for me  that is  it it's very painful for me to say that  part of the reason that i joined the  labour party my party my family started  supporting the party  um was because it was an anti-racist  party  and i think the failure to deal with the  racism that is anti-semitism  is particular and clearly is a problem  and like i mean  you know it's a sadness to listen to you  talk about what has happened and  transpired over the summer  because we failed to deal with this  issue properly most recently  um whether or not we adopt the  international holocaust memorial  alliance's definition  of anti-semitism which we should have  done right at the beginning of the  summer  if had we done that we could have talked  about the big issues  where we had new information being  exposed around climate change around  social care  around the fact that we're still seeing  wages stagnate after the global  financial growth  those are the kinds of issues in  addition of course to brexit that we  should have been talking about in the  summer would say that they have now  adopted that definition in labour's just  been talking saying that they are  going through any complaints about it  you put to him the point that this has  taken months and months and months and  months and they're still outstanding  complaints  there have been reports um you know this  week  that there have been threats of violence  against members of parliament which the  labour party has known of but has not  told members of parliament which is  which is extraordinary so the whole way  that this issue  is being dealt with has been clearly  deficient  and the leadership have acknowledged  that to be the case  and like as you said you're still  talking about it right now i've got to  ask you  if you think labour is institutionally  racist  why are you still in the party because i  want it to change  and look there's institutional racism in  many institutions in our country  and if you simply leave the field as it  were  instead of try and argue and see change  through in an organization  but i'm not sure you necessarily always  make progress and that's  that's the reason why um it's a question  we normally ask people on social media  you've got any questions for your guests  and that was a question that came up  again  and again to ask you why it is that you  are still in the party here's gareth  phillips for example  why won't you quick set the labour party  as he knew it was finished  he and those like him have a  responsibility now to form a new party  so can you categorically rule out  leaving the labour party i don't want to  leave the labour party you rule it out  though  well who knows what i'll be doing when  i'm 80. look i've been a member of the  labour party for more than 20 years  and i joined with the intention of never  leaving it i wanted to be a member of  the labour party on my  deathbed and i hope that that is the  case but ultimately this issue what i'd  say to people and lots of people ask me  this all over the country i was in  birmingham this week and  it came up actually just randomly as you  do when you're going around  and lots of people ask this question but  really i'm not the person to ask this  question of  the point of my speech yesterday was to  say actually ultimately  the job of leadership of the leadership  of the labour party  is to keep the party united and together  and to illustrate not just by what they  say but what they do  that they see the center-left social  democratic tradition in the  labour party as legitimate and by the  actions  both of supporters and members of  different traditions in the labour party  we see that they don't think the  tradition  that i belong to social democracy  center-left  labour values doesn't belong in the  party so it's for them to show  that it does untold numbers of people  have said that they don't feel welcome  in my tradition we've been clear about  how we feel  what are they actually going to do so  this issue of division splits etc it's  not an issue for me  that's the job of leaders to keep a  party together now i want to talk to you  about uh brexit  and this week we'll have a watch at the  film later on in the show um i went to  ghoul a place where  people voted very heavily to leave the  eu  and your campaign for people's vote or a  second referendum on the final deal  however you wish to  phrase it is going down shall we say  pretty badly  in places like one woman for example  saying to me that people like you think  that  she's thick that she needs to think  again and she said to me well i've  already done my thinking and  she's got a point hasn't she no look  it's nothing to do with whether i think  any  what i think of anybody's intelligence  that's a complete irrelevance to me  what matters here is the national  interest and the material circumstances  of the lady that you're  talking about i think what we do know  our country is still divided on this  as you have no doubt discovered and  we'll see in your package later  but i do think there is a shift i'm not  going to pretend that it's a huge shift  but i think there is a shift  certainly increasingly we see polls  illustrating that people want  a say on the final brexit deal that's  very clear now what they do after that  is perhaps a bit more questionable but  the point is i suppose however you voted  in 2016  nobody voted for this chaos and mess and  there are many things we now know that  we didn't know then that we  for example will have to pay a 40  billion pound divorce bill  uh for example that there isn't going to  be that 350 million pounds extra per  week going to the nhs  and in fact what we've got is eu doctors  and nurses eu citizen  doctors and nurses leaving the nhs in  droves when we've got over 100 000  vacancies  which need to be filled and we also see  businesses panasonic's a good example is  moving its headquarters  away from the uk beginning to move their  operations  to the continent now that's before we  have even left  and i think also wherever you are on the  argument  the idea that we leave and go to this  unknown world  you know a kind of blindfolded brexit  where we kind of leave but we don't know  where we're going to  troubles people um massively so i think  that's  where people are there is this real  sense  okay and maybe i voted for this but my  gosh i didn't vote for this mess  are you not playing a pretty high stakes  game here by not  backing the chequers deal that's on the  table because by not backing the  chequers deal you're effectively  maybe making the uh chances of no brexit  more likely but you're also making the  chances of no  deal more likely i mean we've seen boris  johnson for example  in the newspapers today with some pretty  choice words for that checkers deal in  for theresa may you're effectively  siding with him well boris johnson is a  disgrace there's this myth that's been  peddled  in relation to brexit that somehow it's  a fight for the people against the elite  but let's be absolutely clear  brexit is a project of the elite for the  elite i mean  boris johnson who argued for this  proposition  has walked off the field of delivery of  what he promised  has gone into his 250 000 pound a year  job  at the telegraph you've got nigel lawson  another big proponent of it  wanting to go and get residency in  france you've got jacob rees-mogg's  investment firm setting up a fund  on the continent because of the fallout  from brexit so  those guys will be all right but the  bank of england figures are telling us  that  on average people are already 900 pounds  a year  worse off as a result of this project so  let's be clear  t